::ALG2_U02_L03_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L03_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>What is the end behavior of f(x) = 2x<sup>4</sup> - 1? {
~both ends fall #That would require a negative leading coefficient.
=both ends rise #Correct. Even degree with positive leading coefficient means both ends rise.
~left falls, right rises #That is odd positive behavior.
~left rises, right falls #That is odd negative behavior.
}

::ALG2_U02_L03_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L03_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>What is the end behavior of f(x) = -3x<sup>5</sup> + x? {
~both ends rise #Odd degree has opposite end behavior.
~left falls, right rises #That is positive odd behavior.
~both ends fall #That is even negative behavior.
=left rises, right falls #Correct. Odd degree with negative leading coefficient rises left and falls right.
}

::ALG2_U02_L03_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L03_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>Which term controls end behavior of 4x<sup>6</sup> - 9x<sup>2</sup> + 3? {
=4x<sup>6</sup> #Correct. The leading term controls end behavior.
~-9x<sup>2</sup> #This is not the highest-degree term.
~3 #The constant does not control end behavior.
~all terms equally #The leading term dominates.
}

::ALG2_U02_L03_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L03_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>What is the degree of f(x) = -7x<sup>3</sup> + 2x<sup>2</sup> - 1? {
~2 #There is a cubic term.
~-7 #This is the leading coefficient.
=3 #Correct. The highest exponent is 3.
~1 #This is the constant value.
}

::ALG2_U02_L03_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L03_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>What is the leading coefficient of f(x) = -7x<sup>3</sup> + 2x<sup>2</sup> - 1? {
=-7 #Correct. The coefficient of the highest-degree term is -7.
~3 #This is the degree.
~2 #This is another coefficient.
~-1 #This is the constant term.
}

